# A Regexp literal with the /n flag and a byte range above \x7f (Ruby's WEBrick writes
# /[\x7f-\xff]/n) is rebuilt without the flag by the lowering, which raises RegexpError
# ("invalid multibyte escape") inside the interpreter: the type checks are skipped with a warning,
# then the program dies with a Ruby backtrace instead of a Sake error.
# Expected: the literal keeps its /n flag (or a static error naming the line).
# Workaround: match non-ASCII bytes with [^\x00-\x7f] on a String.b copy.
p(String.gsub(String.b("aé"), /[\x7f-\xff]/n) { |m| format("%%%02X", String.ord(m)) })
